1. What are some of the projects you've brought into your classes that deal with our climate crisis or on sustainability or regenerative practices?

- Material Ecologies...
- Post3Rs (Reduce, Reuse, Recycle as design prompts)
- Drawdown Signals...
- What do you want to Sustain (and other thinking/dematerialized projects)
- Individual Student projects... ?

!! Material Ecologies

Basically just trying to account for the material, resource, energy, labor, and capital flows of a given project... I'm not totally concerned with if they get actual accounting figures; but I am concerned with getting our students to see the interconnectedness of all things and how complex the networks are for making something that seems fairly simple, like a book or a poster... it gets especially interesting when you ask a student to think about apps -- like how many apps are on a person's phone? is your app responsible then for just that fraction of the energy/materials/human labor for that phone? what about the servers that apple or google run where app store purchases come from? The average person isn't asked to think that way about their purchases, choices, etc.

!! The Three R's (sometimes I call it Post3Rs, since the output is often poster-y stuff)

Its sort of three days of design charettes based on the "3 Rs"

The first day students are asked to take a project and ponder how a design can be "reduced" -- strategies for lessening material and energy -- can you reduce the colors or the trim size or the file size or the content? and then that becomes a good way to have a conversation about the de materialization of objects and what other forms of communicating could this design take? Its also an opportunity to talk about minimalism not as an aesthetic, but as a formal tool for minimizing material in a design for embedded energy's sake; get into the dieter rams less but better... and start asking the question "do you need to make these things at all!!??"

So we reduce one day, and then the next class they bring in a bunch of old posters, books, preferably their own designs, but it can be any found materials...

we then make "reused" posters conveying some message they want to reach people with... I'm defining reuse as Strategies for making solutions last longer & finding other uses when finished. This is more about thinking about literally physically reusing design to make new design; but it also always turns into a conversation about more general ways of reusing -- frameworks; tools; color palettes; etc.

And then the last part is Recycle; Its important to make the distinction between reuse and recycling -- reuse means you resuse materials in their present state. Recycle means you return materials to a virgin state... Reuse should be less work/energy than recycling. So by recycling for the last day I mean Strategies for reclaiming as much residual value as possible & preventing virgin materials from needlessly being used -- returning used materials back into raw materials. This is an interesting design prompt -- and I go about this by asking the students to try and break down the last few projects they've made for other classes -- what content, images, typefaces, etc. did they create/make? what stuff did they make/create that they didn't end up using? and then we create a giant digital pile of all that stuff and they have to make new things from the old -- its still a little reuse-y but I think by trying to get them to conceptually break the things down to their "raw materials" we have some interesting conversations about graphic design, our materials, our semantic choices, the symbolism of things; and what really is the "raw material" of communicating anyway... is it just signs on substrates?

!! Drawdown Signals

I'm also trying to work with students on seeing the solutions from [[Project Drawdown|drawdown.org]] as opportunities to frame their work under. A colleague of mine is working on some crosswalk designs; so, by making the cross walks more noticeable, they become better signals for walking in the city; thus they aid in Drawdown's "Walkable City" solution... even if they arent a panacea for making cities more walkable... I've assigned this idea to my students: pick a solution or two from drawdown that you are drawn too; I've got a student doing something similar with finding solutions the path that the Jones Falls takes (sorry, the jones falls was a river that flowed through Baltimore; it was buried under the city in the early 1900s, and then the main freeway overpass out of the city is now built over that as well... ) anyway, this student is trying to visualize where the jones falls goes under the city to try and bring awareness back to this lost natural resource... so this ends up being a way to get people into water distribution; coastal wetlands; afforestation... Its about how a symbolic gesture even if small can still help push towards some idea; or more easily make that idea understandable or show a small piece of what really this bigger idea is getting at...

!! What do you want to sustain???

I also assign a lot of thinking projects. We talk about how sustainability doesn't really mean anything until you define what is being sustained... so one of their projects? define what it is that they want to sustain! Or I've taken some questions posed in Teaching as a Subversive Activity by Neil Postman; and I've been rewriting them around climate change urgency instead -- so basically trying to get the students to decide what is important to know about climate change and what their contexts are for understanding and interacting with it; by encouraging students to ask these types of questions; where I might not have the answers; we create an environment of us all trying to solve and do things together; not just me telling them what is the right framework to utilize... maybe the natural step can't solve all problems; maybe knowing about biomimicry isn't really helpful in this situation what do you need to know about instead???

My main class hours are devoted to helping run the fairly open senior studios we have here at MICA -- so while I assign prompts, the prompts are more meant to give students opportunities to make things based on their interests over outlining a specific way of making or underlying ethic or core value... I'm trying to reframe this for myself as well; how can we get every senior to someone cross over into social or eco justice? environmental restoration? just how can sustainability and social practice get into every project??? I do end up with students working in this space -- I had a cool project from a student last year; Calvin Hutcheon; build out a speculative project around the idea of analyzing the health of lichen around an industrial site... anyway, it involved AI, crypto currency, environmental health, cybernetics, automated trading, all with graphic design as the shell that sort of wrapped up everythign together...

I guess that's really what's important is starting to see graphic design as this liminal connection tool, not necessarily the thing that is the solution, but the thing that brings together the people and information to allow solutions to be found, to materialize, not to just create more things...

2. How are your students responding to our climate crisis? How do they see this? As a crisis or as an opportunity?

Its a mixed bag. There are students who actively see it as a mobilizing opportunity. There is a dedicated group of students that are working with the campus and the city on various projects towards making MICA's campus more socially and environmentally equitable … and they seem to also be able to turn their practices towards this as well making work in ways or about subjects that are in themselves activism...

There are some who just want to know more; and don't necessarily see it as a crisis, but understand that they need to know something about this either because they see the trend going that way, or that they want to do something, but don't think it is imperative

This isn't a good short answer. I think many see it as an opportunity.

But they are also seeing a crisis of curriculum -- the existing class and project and pedagogy structures graphic design has built aren't really great at helping students see that doing something about these issues should be part of graphic designing...

Anyway, we have a climate crisis; and we have a design curriculum crisis.

There are starting to be students asking about how to integrate more of these things into more courses; or forcibly bringing content in this area to all their other project prompts...

But man, in general, students aren't unlike the rest of our culture/society -- sort of inert to the whole thing.
